; Engine configuration file.
;
; A stranger's project, not this repo's. No autoloads, no bus layout, no assets,
; no host scripts — the point is what the addon does in a project that did
; nothing for it (ADR-0194, CONTEXT.md "Stranger project"). Shape copied from
; `tests/stranger/exmateria_schema/project.godot`; this is that rig pointed at a
; second in-walk addon, not a second invention.
;
; THE HARNESS FILES KEEP THEIR REPO-RELATIVE PATHS in the staged project —
; `res://tests/stranger/<addon>/…`, not `res://…` at the root — so every quoted
; `res://` literal is true in BOTH projects and `tools/check_res_paths.py` stays
; green on a clean checkout. See the schema rig's project.godot for the full note.
;
; THE ADDON IS NOT ENABLED HERE, and that is deliberate — but since ADR-0220 it is
; no longer true that the addon installs NOTHING. Its `plugin.gd` still registers no
; autoload and no singleton, and a consumer uses it by naming `ExMateriaPlatform` —
; the addon's ONE global name since ADR-0212 dec. 1, carrying `TunePort`,
; `DisplayPort` and `PsxNum` as constants — by resolving `PSXDisplay` at the node
; path the host registers it on, and by `#include`ing its `.gdshaderinc` seams. A
; project that did nothing for it must still be able to do exactly that, and that is
; what the un-enabled state measures.
;
; WHAT IT DOES INSTALL is six `[shader_globals]` entries, one for each `global
; uniform` it declares (ADR-0220 dec. 1/2). `PlatformProvidesTest.tscn` is the
; oracle for that write, and it CALLS `provide_into` against a double rather than
; waiting for an enable — which is the only way to reach it, here or in the host,
; since neither project has an `[editor_plugins]` section. So this rig measures both
; halves: the seams work un-enabled, and the provide is real when it runs.

config_version=5

[application]

config/name="stranger_exmateria_platform"
run/main_scene="res://tests/stranger/shared/stranger_install.tscn"
config/features=PackedStringArray("4.4")

[audio]

driver/driver="Dummy"
